menu "Debug Options"

choice
    prompt "Log level"
    default OVE_LOG_LEVEL_INF

config OVE_LOG_LEVEL_ERR
    bool "Error only"

config OVE_LOG_LEVEL_WRN
    bool "Warning and above"

config OVE_LOG_LEVEL_INF
    bool "Info and above"

config OVE_LOG_LEVEL_DBG
    bool "Debug (all)"

endchoice

config OVE_LOG_LEVEL
    int
    default 0 if OVE_LOG_LEVEL_ERR
    default 1 if OVE_LOG_LEVEL_WRN
    default 2 if OVE_LOG_LEVEL_INF
    default 3 if OVE_LOG_LEVEL_DBG

config OVE_DEBUG_BUILD
    bool "Debug build (no optimization, debug symbols)"
    default n

config OVE_STACK_CANARIES
    bool "Stack canary protection (-fstack-protector-strong)"
    default n
    help
      Compile the project's own sources (the module + backends + app — the code
      that handles untrusted guest input; not the vendored HAL/RTOS trees) with
      -fstack-protector-strong. A function with a stack buffer gets a canary
      checked before it returns; a host stack smash then halts via
      __stack_chk_fail instead of executing a corrupted return address.

      On STM32 the canary base is seeded from the hardware RNG at boot, before any
      guest runs, so it is unpredictable and — being host memory behind the MPU —
      unreadable by a guest. A detected smash prints a diagnostic and halts; a
      watchdog, if enabled, reboots.

      Defence-in-depth for the host (the MPU already isolates guests). Opt-in:
      adds a small per-function prologue/epilogue in flash and cycles.

config OVE_THREAD_STATE_STATS
    bool "Per-thread state time tracking"
    default y if OVE_SIM
    help
      Track cumulative time each thread spends in each state
      (running, ready, blocked, suspended). Adds a timestamp
      on every state transition. Enables per-state % display
      in the sim dashboard thread view.

config OVE_TRACE_STREAM
    bool "Thread-state trace streaming"
    depends on OVE_THREAD_STATE_STATS
    default y if OVE_SIM
    help
      Emit every thread state transition as a timestamped record
      into a lock-free multi-producer ring buffer, drained by the sim
      transport layer and rendered as a swimlane in the dashboard
      trace panel. Disabling removes all per-transition overhead.

config OVE_TRACE_MARKERS
    bool "Sync-primitive trace markers"
    depends on OVE_TRACE_STREAM
    default y
    help
      In addition to state transitions, emit point markers when
      threads enter/leave sync primitives (mutex, semaphore, event,
      condvar, queue). Renders as triangles on the trace swimlane,
      aligned with known sem_take/sem_give sites.

      Performance note: each marker is two wrapper calls plus a
      CAS ring push (~100 ns) around every sync op. On a fast host
      this roughly doubled-to-tripled the cost of cheap uncontended
      primitives (mutex_lock_unlock 70->175 ns, sem_take_give
      71->210 ns in the C benchmark). Workloads with
      tight sync-primitive loops should leave this disabled;
      state transitions are still captured via OVE_TRACE_STREAM.

config OVE_TRACE_RING_SIZE
    int "Trace ring buffer size (records)"
    depends on OVE_TRACE_STREAM
    default 4096
    help
      Capacity of the trace ring buffer, in records (16 bytes each,
      plus a 1-byte commit flag). On overflow the producer drops
      records and increments a counter reported by the dashboard.
      4096 records = 68 KiB, ~30 s of typical activity at 150
      transitions/s.

config OVE_PROFILER
    bool "Sampling profiler"
    depends on OVE_SIM
    depends on OVE_THREAD_STATE_STATS
    default y
    help
      Periodically samples thread PCs + call stacks and forwards them
      to the dashboard for flat top-N and flame-graph rendering.
      FreeRTOS samples the interrupted task from a dedicated hardware
      timer (CMSDK TIMER1 on MPS2-AN500) and walks saved-{r7,lr} pairs
      out of its stack; Zephyr does the same from a k_timer expiry and
      shares the walker (backends/common/ove_arm_backtrace.c). Both
      jitter each period by +/-25% so sampling cannot lock to the
      kernel tick or to periodic work. NuttX walks every thread with
      up_backtrace(tcb) from a watchdog-timed high-priority work item
      (CONFIG_SCHED_BACKTRACE, set in the generated NuttX defconfig).

      Needs OVE_THREAD_STATE_STATS, which provides the sample time
      source and the per-sample thread state.

config OVE_PROFILER_HZ
    int "Sampling frequency (Hz)"
    depends on OVE_PROFILER
    default 250
    range 10 1000
    help
      Mean sample rate. Higher values give finer resolution at the
      cost of overhead (one stack walk per sample; on NuttX one walk
      per thread). Zephyr and NuttX cap it at the kernel tick rate.
      250 Hz is a good default for interactive workloads.

config OVE_PROFILER_MAX_DEPTH
    int "Maximum stack depth per sample"
    depends on OVE_PROFILER
    default 32
    range 4 64
    help
      Upper bound on frames captured per sample. Deeper stacks are
      truncated; LVGL rendering chains run about 30 frames deep. Each
      sample allocates a fixed slot of header + depth * sizeof(void *)
      bytes in the ring.

config OVE_PROFILER_RING_SIZE
    int "Profiler sample ring size (samples)"
    depends on OVE_PROFILER
    default 1024
    help
      Capacity of the profiler sample ring. Each slot is
      16 B header + MAX_DEPTH * sizeof(void *). On overflow
      samples are dropped and counted.

endmenu
